```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r11
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies in R11's OSPF redistribution configuration, not in any physical
interface failure as the maintenance team suspected. R11 is the EIGRP-to-OSPF
boundary router: its eth0 (112.x, segment EE) faces the EIGRP cloud containing
R12/R13/R10 and pc12's 120.0.0.0/8 LAN, while its eth1 (111.x, segment B) faces
the OSPF backbone via a fully established Full/Backup adjacency with R1.

All three of R11's interfaces are physically UP/LOWER_UP, and both routing
protocols are healthy: OSPF neighbor 111.1.1.1 (R1) is in Full state, and EIGRP
neighbors 112.1.1.2 (R12) and 103.1.1.2 (R13) are both active. This directly
refutes the "boundary link unplugged" theory.

The actual mechanism: pc12 (120.1.1.x) sends traffic toward the OSPF backbone
using R12's kernel default route (0.0.0.0/0 → R11), which delivers packets to R11,
which then forwards them via its OSPF-learned routes. However, return traffic from
any OSPF backbone router toward 120.0.0.0/8 is silently dropped: R1's routing
table shows "% Network not in table" for 120.0.0.0/8, because R11's OSPF process
contains only `redistribute connected` and never injects EIGRP-learned prefixes
(including 120.0.0.0/8) into OSPF. Asymmetric routing — forward path works,
return path is black-holed.

Root cause: R11's `router ospf` block is missing the `redistribute eigrp 100`
statement, which was apparently removed during the maintenance window.

Corrective fix: On R11, under `router ospf`, add `redistribute eigrp 100` (with
an appropriate route-map if metric filtering is desired). This will inject
120.0.0.0/8 and other EIGRP prefixes into OSPF, giving backbone routers a return
path to pc12's subnet and restoring full bidirectional reachability.
=== END ===
